## Checkpoint 2 — Call site

Three candidates for the primary API.

### Candidate A — One primitive, `ctx.bindStore(store, apply)`

```ts
interface ReadableStore<T> {
  get(): T;
  subscribe(fn: (s: T) => void): () => void;
}

ctx.bindStore<T>(store: ReadableStore<T>, apply: (s: T) => void): void;
```

Replaces the (apply, subscribe, onUpdate-reapply) triplet with one line:

```ts
// reader_settings.ts — ReaderStylesHook
ctx.bindStore(readerSettings, (s) => {
  ctx.el.style.maxWidth = `${s.maxWidth}px`;
  // …
});

// reader_settings.ts — ReaderSettingsControlsHook  (auto-next bug fixed)
ctx.bindStore(readerSettings, syncControls);

// audio_player.ts — volume/collapsed/speed
ctx.bindStore(playerPrefs, (p) => {
  audio.volume = p.volume;
  audio.playbackRate = p.speed;
  if (volSlider) volSlider.value = String(p.volume);
  ctx.el.classList.toggle("collapsed", p.collapsed);
});
```

**Awkward:** the play/pause icon's source is `audio.paused`, not a store. To use `bindStore` we'd have to invent a derived store or fall back to hand-wiring `ctx.on(audio, "play", …) + ctx.on(audio, "pause", …) + ctx.onUpdate(…) + initial call`. The four-line ceremony remains, the bug class isn't fully closed.

### Candidate B — Two primitives, sources named for what they are

```ts
interface ReadableStore<T> {
  get(): T;
  subscribe(fn: (s: T) => void): () => void;
}

ctx.bindStore<T>(store: ReadableStore<T>, apply: (s: T) => void): void;

ctx.bindElement<K extends keyof HTMLElementEventMap>(
  target: HTMLElement,
  events: readonly K[],
  apply: () => void,
): void;
```

Both run `apply` immediately, register cleanup on destroy, and re-apply on every `ctx.onUpdate`. Adds the auto-next/font-size/play-pause-icon fix in three lines:

```ts
// reader_settings.ts — ReaderStylesHook
ctx.bindStore(readerSettings, (s) => { /* style apply */ });

// reader_settings.ts — ReaderSettingsControlsHook  (auto-next & slider bugs fixed)
ctx.bindStore(readerSettings, syncControls);

// audio_player.ts — store-driven prefs (volume slider bug fixed pre-emptively)
ctx.bindStore(playerPrefs, (p) => {
  audio.volume = p.volume;
  audio.playbackRate = p.speed;
  if (volSlider) volSlider.value = String(p.volume);
  ctx.el.classList.toggle("collapsed", p.collapsed);
});

// audio_player.ts — play/pause icon  (icon bug fixed)
ctx.bindElement(audio, ["play", "pause"], () => {
  playPauseBtn.innerHTML = audio.paused ? PLAY_GLYPH : PAUSE_GLYPH;
});
```

**Awkward:** two methods instead of one; mild surface bloat. But each is named for its source; a whole-codebase scan finds zero sites that need a third source kind, so the design is closed (Move 8 — flexible at the seam, opinionated inside).

### Candidate C — One unified primitive, `ctx.bind(source, apply)` polymorphic over store-or-element-event

```ts
ctx.bind<T>(source: BindSource<T>, apply: (snapshot: T) => void): void;
// BindSource<T> = ReadableStore<T> | { element: HTMLElement; events: readonly string[] }
```

**Awkward:** the projection callback shape doesn't unify cleanly — element-event sources have no snapshot value, so `apply` becomes `(s: T | void) => void` or the type splits via overload. The unification is cosmetic and hides a real shape difference.

### Pick: **Candidate B.**

- **Move 1:** call-site reads as "match this DOM to this store" / "this DOM to this element's events." Both discoverable via `ctx.bind…` autocomplete.
- **Move 2:** both implicit concepts named.
- **Move 7:** smaller public surface than today (`readerSettings.subscribe` becomes hook-internal once the wrapper collapses to a `PersistedRecord`).
- **Move 8:** `ReadableStore<T>` is the right seam — `readerSettings`, `playerPrefs`, `scrollFollow` all plug in. `apply` is opinionated inline, no speculative generality.

It closes the bug class: a new client-bound control can't ship without using one of the two primitives, and both bake in the morphdom re-sync.

**Stop. Awaiting approval to proceed to Checkpoint 3 (itch list).**

